    "title": "Cardinal News reported on the heat wave and drought in Virginia",
    "text": "5 hours ago Heat Wave Photo by Luis Graterol on Unsplash Cardinal News is an independent, nonprofit, and nonpartisan digital news organization dedicated to in-depth journalism in Southwest and Southside Virginia. Cardinal News keeps Virginians updated on many things that affect them, including the prolonged heat wave .\nOn Wednesday, July 8, Cardinal News reported on the heat wave that has been sweeping across the state, prompting health advisories, cooling center openings, and a surge in tick-borne illnesses.\nCardinal News wrote:\n\"The heat wave is over, but it's still July, so the heat isn't. The worst heat wave in 14 years has passed. But don’t break out the sweaters. There’s a lot more sweating left. It is, after all, July. We are going to have lots of “regular” hot, sticky days in which some temperatures pop over 90. There might even be something of a resurgence of higher heat in a week or so.\" The outlet said that June 28 to July 9, 2012 was the last time it was even hotter and for longer in the Southwest and Southside Virginia region.\nResult of a Drought Photo by Pawel Czerwinski on Unsplash Cardinal News also reported on the ongoing drought . That seems odd because Virginia had several downpours last week. According to National Weather Service data, the region needs 8 to 12 inches of rain in a month to entirely resolve the drought.\n1",
